Protocol 
A set of rules that devices follow to communicate.
Just like: Humans follow grammar rules to speak.
           Computers follow protocol rules to send/receive data.
Without protocols → communication fails 

Why Protocols Are Important 
       Protocols define:
             How data is formatted
             How data is transmitted
             How errors are handled
             How security is maintained
             How connection is established & closed

Protocol Layers (Important Concept): Protocols work in layers.

OSI Model (7 Layers)
Layer	        What Happens	         Example               Protocol
7             Application	           User interaction	     HTTP, FTP, SSH
6             Presentation	         Encryption	           TLS/SSL
5             Session	               Session control	     NetBIOS
4             Transport	             End-to-end delivery	 TCP, UDP
3             Network	               Routing	             IP
2             Data Link            	 MAC address	         ARP
1             Physical	             Cables, signals	     Ethernet

Networking Protocols : 

1. IP – Internet Protocol - IP is responsible for logical addressing and routing, but it is unreliable and connectionless.
        Layer: Network Layer
        Purpose: Provides logical addressing for devices.
   What it does: Assigns unique IP address
                 Routes packets between networks
                 Does NOT guarantee delivery
   Types: IPv4 and IPv6
          IP only delivers packet — it doesn’t guarantee delivery.


2. TCP – Transmission Control Protocol - TCP is connection-oriented and guarantees reliable delivery of data.
         Layer: Transport Layer
         Purpose : Port-based communication
         What it does: Reliable data transfer
                       Ensures packet order
                       Error detection & retransmission
         Uses 3-way handshake
         Used in: HTTP, HTTPS, SSH, FTP

3. UDP – User Datagram Protocol -UDP is connectionless and faster but does not guarantee reliability.
         Layer: Transport Layer
         What it does:Fast transmission
                      No delivery guarantee
                      No order checking
        Used in: Video streaming, Online gaming, DNS queries

4. HTTP – HyperText Transfer Protocol - HTTP is a stateless application layer protocol used for web communication.
          Port: 80
          Layer: Application Layer
          What it does:Transfers web pages
                       Stateless protocol

5. HTTPS – Secure HTTP - HTTPS ensures secure communication using SSL/TLS encryption.
           Port: 443
           HTTP + SSL/TLS encryption
           What it does: Encrypts web traffic
                         Secure data transfer

6. DNS – Domain Name System - DNS resolves human-readable domain names into IP addresses.
         Port: 53 (Mostly UDP)
         What it does: Converts domain name → IP address
                       Example: google.com → 142.x.x.x

7. SSH – Secure Shell - DNS resolves human-readable domain names into IP addresses.
         Port: 22
         What it does:Secure remote login
                      Encrypted communication
         Used in: Server management, Git, DevOps automation

8. FTP – File Transfer Protocol - FTP is used for file transfer but is insecure unless secured with SFTP/FTPS.
         Port: 21
         What it does: Transfers files between systems
                       Not encrypted by default
        Secure versions: SFTP, FTPS

9. SMTP – Simple Mail Transfer Protocol - SMTP is an application layer protocol used to send emails over a network.
          Port: 25
          What it does: Sends emails between servers

10. ARP – Address Resolution Protocol - ARP resolves IP addresses to physical MAC addresses in a LAN.
          Port: 0
          What it does: Converts IP address → MAC address, Works inside local network

TCP vs UDP (Very Important)
          TCP	                 UDP
          Reliable	           Unreliable
          Slower	             Faster
          Connection-oriented	 Connectionless
          Used in banking	     Used in streaming

